<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Essen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Essen is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew around Essen Abbey, a community of noble canonesses founded in the 9th century whose princess-abbesses ruled the town until 1803. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and armaments works made it one of the industrial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its former abbey church became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen-cathedral">Essen Cathedral</a> in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/essen.jpg" alt="Essen" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Essen_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
